## Hints for Future Agents
The filter is the point, not the line count. "Would a competent model do the
wrong thing without this" over-cuts, because a competent model does something
merely acceptable instead of what Aaron wants — a416b36 learned this the hard
way. The test that worked: does this encode a particular opinion, or is it
generic advice the model already has? Generic goes even when correct. Stance
stays even when a good model would manage without it.

comments.md's final paragraph is the only genuinely new content in this commit,
and it is the thing Aaron actually came for. Do not compress it away as
redundant with "write no comments by default." It solves a different problem:
comments that reference the session's own history rather than the code's state.
The design decision inside it is a REDIRECT, not a prohibition — session history
goes to the commit note. That wording was chosen because a prohibition leaves the
impulse looking for another outlet (variable names like newHandler, tests named
after fixed bugs, dead code kept "for reference"), and those are named explicitly
for the same reason.

The ownership rule in that paragraph resolves a real conflict: "do not strip
existing comments as a side effect of unrelated work" (inherited comments are
Chesterton's Fences) versus "delete the ones you wrote earlier in the session"
(yours to clean up). The distinguishing question is who wrote it, and it is
checkable. Do not collapse the two halves.

Aaron chose ONE always-on writing file over the rule-plus-skill split, with the
reason: "I want the writing style to be consistent no matter what, not only
conditionally applied." The 1745fbc split is therefore settled against, not
merely untried. Do not propose it a third time without new evidence.

subtractive-engineering.md:28 references `rules/simple.md` for the fence
procedure. simple.md's fence paragraph exists to satisfy that reference. If you
compress simple.md further, check that reference first.

## Codebase Discoveries
~/.claude/rules is a symlink to this repo's rules/, so every edit in this session
was live in Aaron's global context before it was committed. There is no way to
stage a rules change for review without it being active.

The harness system prompt for this session already contains the post's
replacement sentence verbatim: "Write code that reads like the surrounding code:
match its comment density, naming, and idiom." That is why comments.md opens with
the repo-convention deference — the two can conflict in a comment-heavy repo, and
the deference resolves it.

The harness task reminder fires on its own and names TaskCreate, TaskUpdate, and
the in_progress/completed lifecycle. That is what made task-tracking.md
deletable despite the Task tools being deferred, which was a416b36's stated
reason for keeping it.

